1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,738
A customer says: your site is broken.

2
00:00:02,738 --> 00:00:04,129
You open the dashboard,

3
00:00:04,129 --> 00:00:05,701
and everything looks fine.

4
00:00:05,701 --> 00:00:07,092
Where do you look next?

5
00:00:07,642 --> 00:00:09,853
Always in the same place: the logs.

6
00:00:09,853 --> 00:00:13,074
Every request engine X ever served is written down,

7
00:00:13,074 --> 00:00:15,663
with a number that tells you how it went.

8
00:00:16,213 --> 00:00:18,327
Today you learn to read that number,

9
00:00:18,327 --> 00:00:20,324
find a failing request in seconds,

10
00:00:20,324 --> 00:00:22,379
and ask the log the right question.

11
00:00:22,929 --> 00:00:24,635
Read the log, trust the number.

12
00:00:26,185 --> 00:00:29,257
Here is what to expect, from the first minute to the last.

13
00:00:29,807 --> 00:00:32,964
One: the two logs, and the families of status codes.

14
00:00:33,514 --> 00:00:37,781
Two: a live demo. We send a mix of requests to a real engine X.

15
00:00:38,331 --> 00:00:40,172
Three: we read the access log,

16
00:00:40,172 --> 00:00:41,154
count the codes,

17
00:00:41,154 --> 00:00:43,792
and chase a five oh two into the error log.

18
00:00:44,342 --> 00:00:46,972
Four: we add the request time to every line.

19
00:00:46,972 --> 00:00:47,868
Then your turn,

20
00:00:47,868 --> 00:00:50,319
with a lab that asks you three questions,

21
00:00:50,319 --> 00:00:52,769
and checks your answers against the logs.

22
00:00:53,319 --> 00:00:56,072
This is lesson five of the web servers domain.

23
00:00:56,072 --> 00:00:57,688
Last time you built a pool.

24
00:00:57,688 --> 00:01:00,082
Today you learn to see what is going on.

25
00:01:00,632 --> 00:01:02,338
Read the log, trust the number.

26
00:01:04,388 --> 00:01:07,737
Two logs, two different questions. Let us see which is which.

27
00:01:08,287 --> 00:01:11,408
The access log has one line per request: who asked,

28
00:01:11,408 --> 00:01:12,631
what they asked for,

29
00:01:12,631 --> 00:01:14,773
and the status code that came back.

30
00:01:15,323 --> 00:01:20,009
The error log is engine X talking about itself: a file that was not found,

31
00:01:20,009 --> 00:01:21,909
a backend that did not answer,

32
00:01:21,909 --> 00:01:23,429
a setting that is wrong.

33
00:01:23,979 --> 00:01:26,194
Status codes come in five families.

34
00:01:26,194 --> 00:01:27,649
Two hundred: it worked.

35
00:01:27,649 --> 00:01:29,737
Three hundred: go somewhere else.

36
00:01:29,737 --> 00:01:33,027
Four hundred: the visitor asked for something wrong.

37
00:01:33,027 --> 00:01:35,051
Five hundred: the server failed.

38
00:01:35,601 --> 00:01:36,803
Remember the split.

39
00:01:36,803 --> 00:01:39,144
Four hundred is the client's problem.

40
00:01:39,144 --> 00:01:40,536
Five hundred is yours.

41
00:01:40,536 --> 00:01:41,674
And a five oh two,

42
00:01:41,674 --> 00:01:42,433
bad gateway,

43
00:01:42,433 --> 00:01:46,545
means engine X could not get an answer from the server behind it.

44
00:01:48,095 --> 00:01:53,215
Go to the lab folder.

45
00:01:54,765 --> 00:01:56,664
Look around. Type: L S.

46
00:01:57,214 --> 00:02:01,566
A site, a config, an empty logs folder, and a script that sends traffic.

47
00:02:02,116 --> 00:02:08,132
Which special locations exist?

48
00:02:08,682 --> 00:02:09,071
Three.

49
00:02:09,071 --> 00:02:11,797
Anything under admin gets a four oh three,

50
00:02:11,797 --> 00:02:12,446
forbidden.

51
00:02:12,446 --> 00:02:15,172
The path old is moved with a three oh one.

52
00:02:15,172 --> 00:02:18,612
And broken is proxied to port eighty-two twenty-nine,

53
00:02:18,612 --> 00:02:20,364
where nothing is listening.

54
00:02:20,364 --> 00:02:22,506
Everything else is a normal page.

55
00:02:23,056 --> 00:02:26,832
Send the traffic.

56
00:02:27,382 --> 00:02:29,255
A fixed mix of good requests,

57
00:02:29,255 --> 00:02:30,160
missing pages,

58
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:31,064
one forbidden,

59
00:02:31,064 --> 00:02:31,904
one redirect,

60
00:02:31,904 --> 00:02:34,230
and two requests to the broken path.

61
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:40,113
Read the first lines of the access log.

62
00:02:40,663 --> 00:02:42,211
Each line is one request.

63
00:02:42,211 --> 00:02:43,388
The client address,

64
00:02:43,388 --> 00:02:44,750
the request in quotes,

65
00:02:44,750 --> 00:02:45,741
the status code,

66
00:02:45,741 --> 00:02:46,855
the size in bytes,

67
00:02:46,855 --> 00:02:48,527
and the program that asked.

68
00:02:48,527 --> 00:02:49,518
Here it is curl.

69
00:02:49,518 --> 00:02:52,738
The number after the quotes is the one that matters.

70
00:02:53,288 --> 00:03:06,280
Count requests per status code.

71
00:03:06,830 --> 00:03:07,889
Six two hundreds,

72
00:03:07,889 --> 00:03:08,948
one three oh one,

73
00:03:08,948 --> 00:03:10,070
one four oh three,

74
00:03:10,070 --> 00:03:11,254
some four oh fours,

75
00:03:11,254 --> 00:03:12,563
and two five oh twos.

76
00:03:12,563 --> 00:03:14,993
Awk picked the fifth word of each line,

77
00:03:14,993 --> 00:03:16,115
sort grouped them,

78
00:03:16,115 --> 00:03:17,299
and unique counted.

79
00:03:17,299 --> 00:03:21,038
The log has told you the shape of the traffic in one second.

80
00:03:21,588 --> 00:03:29,502
Count the 404s.

81
00:03:30,052 --> 00:03:34,212
That number is the four oh fours. Remember it, it is your first answer.

82
00:03:34,762 --> 00:03:42,058
Show the 502s.

83
00:03:42,608 --> 00:03:44,524
Both on the same path: broken.

84
00:03:44,524 --> 00:03:47,206
Four hundreds are mistakes of the visitor.

85
00:03:47,206 --> 00:03:49,823
Five hundreds are failures of the system.

86
00:03:49,823 --> 00:03:51,675
This one needs the error log.

87
00:03:52,225 --> 00:04:06,454
Ask the error log why.

88
00:04:07,004 --> 00:04:08,264
Connection refused,

89
00:04:08,264 --> 00:04:09,458
error one one one.

90
00:04:09,458 --> 00:04:11,845
Engine X tried to reach the backend,

91
00:04:11,845 --> 00:04:13,503
and nobody was listening.

92
00:04:13,503 --> 00:04:16,023
That is exactly what a five oh two is.

93
00:04:16,023 --> 00:04:17,482
Without the error log,

94
00:04:17,482 --> 00:04:21,063
the access log only says what; the error log says why.

95
00:04:21,613 --> 00:04:26,882
Open the config in vim.

96
00:04:27,432 --> 00:04:28,483
Go to line nine,

97
00:04:28,483 --> 00:04:29,795
the access log line,

98
00:04:29,795 --> 00:04:33,209
and make it end with the word timed instead of main.

99
00:04:33,209 --> 00:04:34,915
Then open a line above it,

100
00:04:34,915 --> 00:04:36,425
and define that format,

101
00:04:36,425 --> 00:04:38,132
called timed: the request,

102
00:04:38,132 --> 00:04:38,854
the status,

103
00:04:38,854 --> 00:04:41,480
and then R T equals dollar request time,

104
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:43,646
the seconds engine X spent on it.

105
00:04:47,096 --> 00:04:53,197
Test, then reload.

106
00:04:53,747 --> 00:04:55,475
Applied without stopping anything.

107
00:04:56,025 --> 00:05:03,598
Empty the old log.

108
00:05:04,148 --> 00:05:08,223
Send the traffic again.

109
00:05:08,773 --> 00:05:14,042
Read the new format.

110
00:05:14,592 --> 00:05:19,008
Now every line ends with R T and a number: how long the request took.

111
00:05:19,008 --> 00:05:21,440
This is how you find the slow request,

112
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:22,912
before a customer does.

113
00:05:23,462 --> 00:05:33,638
Write answer one.

114
00:05:34,188 --> 00:05:41,058
Write answer two.

115
00:05:41,608 --> 00:05:48,477
Write answer three.

116
00:05:49,027 --> 00:05:52,120
Read the answers.

117
00:05:52,670 --> 00:05:56,660
A number, a path, and a word. Three facts, all found in the logs.

118
00:05:57,210 --> 00:05:59,578
Run the judge.

119
00:06:00,128 --> 00:06:00,889
Five checks.

120
00:06:00,889 --> 00:06:02,476
The new format is in use.

121
00:06:02,476 --> 00:06:05,078
The log holds the traffic in that format.

122
00:06:05,078 --> 00:06:09,139
And each of your three answers is checked against the real logs.

123
00:06:09,139 --> 00:06:10,218
Five out of five.

124
00:06:10,768 --> 00:06:12,731
Clean up.

125
00:06:13,281 --> 00:06:16,652
Engine X stopped, and everything removed, logs included.

126
00:06:17,202 --> 00:06:21,682
Is the port free? Count the lines.

127
00:06:22,232 --> 00:06:25,240
Zero. Nothing listens, nothing is left behind.

128
00:06:26,790 --> 00:06:30,907
Your task. Read the logs, and answer three questions from the evidence.

129
00:06:31,457 --> 00:06:35,831
One: lab up, run the traffic script, and count the requests per status code.

130
00:06:36,381 --> 00:06:40,242
Two: find the five oh twos, and read the error log to learn why.

131
00:06:40,792 --> 00:06:46,659
Three: define a log format with request time, and use it in access log. Test, and reload.

132
00:06:47,209 --> 00:06:51,091
Four: empty the log, send the traffic again, and read the new lines.

133
00:06:51,641 --> 00:06:56,761
Five: write three answers into answers dot text, run lab check, then lab down.

134
00:06:58,811 --> 00:07:02,587
Here is where to find everything. Pause on this screen, and write it down.

135
00:07:03,137 --> 00:07:04,785
The lab lives on GitHub,

136
00:07:04,785 --> 00:07:05,952
at it dash waves,

137
00:07:05,952 --> 00:07:07,532
slash dev ops dash A I,

138
00:07:07,532 --> 00:07:09,042
in the folder academy,

139
00:07:09,042 --> 00:07:09,729
slash lab.

140
00:07:10,279 --> 00:07:15,868
Two files: lab one twenty-two dot S H, and shortcuts one twenty-two dot S H.

141
00:07:16,418 --> 00:07:17,319
Install it,

142
00:07:17,319 --> 00:07:20,184
on any Linux machine: sudo install,

143
00:07:20,184 --> 00:07:22,066
dash m seven five five,

144
00:07:22,066 --> 00:07:24,277
lab one twenty-two dot S H,

145
00:07:24,277 --> 00:07:25,504
into slash usr,

146
00:07:25,504 --> 00:07:26,487
slash local,

147
00:07:26,487 --> 00:07:27,469
slash S bin.

148
00:07:28,019 --> 00:07:32,758
Load the short names with: source shortcuts one twenty-two dot S H.

149
00:07:32,758 --> 00:07:33,960
You get two: lab,

150
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:34,526
and N G.

151
00:07:35,076 --> 00:07:37,522
You need engine X and curl installed,

152
00:07:37,522 --> 00:07:39,902
and port eighty-two twenty-one free.

153
00:07:39,902 --> 00:07:42,414
The lab runs its own private engine X.

154
00:07:42,964 --> 00:07:44,679
Lab up prepares everything.

155
00:07:44,679 --> 00:07:46,394
Lab check judges your work.

156
00:07:46,394 --> 00:07:47,918
Lab down removes it all,

157
00:07:47,918 --> 00:07:49,124
including the logs.

158
00:07:49,124 --> 00:07:51,156
Only run it on your own machine.

159
00:07:53,206 --> 00:07:55,275
Pass or fail. You need all five.

160
00:07:55,825 --> 00:07:58,748
Access log uses a format with request time.

161
00:07:59,298 --> 00:08:01,730
The log holds the full traffic in that format.

162
00:08:02,280 --> 00:08:04,243
Your count of four oh fours is right.

163
00:08:04,793 --> 00:08:06,947
You named the path behind the five oh two.

164
00:08:07,497 --> 00:08:09,417
You named the reason in the error log.

165
00:08:09,967 --> 00:08:11,269
To pass, all five.

166
00:08:13,319 --> 00:08:15,090
Your turn, on your own machine.

167
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:19,330
One: lab up, run the traffic script, and count the codes with awk.

168
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:23,486
Two: find the five oh twos, and the reason in the error log.

169
00:08:24,036 --> 00:08:27,534
Three: add the new log format in vim. Test, and reload.

170
00:08:28,084 --> 00:08:31,306
Four: empty the log, send the traffic again, and read it.

171
00:08:31,856 --> 00:08:36,784
Five: write answers dot text, and run lab check until you see five out of five.

172
00:08:37,334 --> 00:08:39,915
Six: lab down, and prove the port is free.

173
00:08:40,465 --> 00:08:42,300
Pause the video, and begin now.

174
00:08:57,850 --> 00:08:58,725
Quick check.

175
00:08:59,275 --> 00:09:03,413
What question does the access log answer, and what does the error log answer?

176
00:09:03,963 --> 00:09:06,630
What do the four hundreds and the five hundreds mean?

177
00:09:07,180 --> 00:09:08,737
What does a five oh two tell you?

178
00:09:09,287 --> 00:09:11,271
Why add the request time to the log?

179
00:09:11,821 --> 00:09:18,712
Objectives covered: M C thirteen, objective six. Access logs, error logs and status codes.

180
00:09:19,262 --> 00:09:21,331
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

181
